<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Sanjak of Alexandretta</h1><p class="st-meta">1921 CE – 1938 CE · capital Alexandretta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Syrian district with a large Turkish population whose status, disputed between France and Turkey, ended in its annexation by Turkey.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ankara-1921">Treaty of Ankara</a> of 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> agreed that the district of Alexandretta, which included <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Antioch</a> and a large Turkish-speaking population alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alawites">Alawites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, should have a special administrative regime within French-ruled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>. It formed an autonomous sanjak attached first to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-aleppo">State of Aleppo</a> and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Turkish was an official language and the sanjak had its own administration. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> promised <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> independence in 1936, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> demanded the district. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> drafted a statute making it a separate entity, and elections held in 1938 under Turkish military presence gave Turks a majority in the assembly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In September 1938 the assembly proclaimed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hatay-state">Hatay State</a>, which joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in 1939. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> has never formally recognised the loss.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sanjak-of-alexandretta.jpg" alt="Sanjak of Alexandretta" width="320" height="265"><figcaption>Image: Don-kun, TUBS, NordNordWest · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_Mandate_for_Syria_and_the_Lebanon_map_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1921 CE – 1938 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alexandretta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Alawite Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Autonomous Sanjak of Alexandretta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=sanjak-of-alexandretta" aria-label="Sanjak of Alexandretta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5592 864 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sanjak of Alexandretta lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5912 1120L5905 1205M5912 1120L5911 1127"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5905 1205h0M5911 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5912 1120h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5912 1120h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=sanjak-of-alexandretta">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
